 This document collects the scientific literature regarding **brain**.                                               
                                                                                                                     
 ## Neural Decoding and Brain-Computer Interfaces                                                                    
                                                                                                                     
-Recent advances in brain-to-language decoding have expanded the field from constrained recognition to text          
generation and streaming personalized speech, synthesizing developments across invasive and non-invasive measurements
while highlighting the need for shared benchmarks and longitudinal studies [^2609.27650]. To address session         
variability in invasive brain-machine interfaces, Task-Conditioned Latent Alignment (TCLA) stabilizes neural decoding
by learning a shared latent space aligned separately for each task condition, significantly reducing failure rates   
across cross-subject and long-term evaluations [^2609.27441]. In the realm of signal preprocessing, attention-guided 
conditional adversarial learning (P2P Trans) effectively suppresses electromyographic artifacts in single-channel    
EEG, demonstrating superior spectral restoration under severe contamination [^2609.26772]. Furthermore, modeling     
long-horizon EEG sequences benefits from neuroscience-inspired tokenization; Brain-Token Learning replaces fixed     
temporal windows with microstate-derived brain tokens, capturing quasi-stable large-scale brain states and improving 
representation learning across affective and clinical datasets [^2609.24324]. Optimizing cross-subject EEG decoding  
remains challenging due to gradient nonstationarity, but adaptive forgetting controllers like AFOR dynamically adjust
second-moment decay coefficients, yielding robust performance improvements over fixed optimizers [^2609.24233].      
Developmental neuroscience also benefits from these techniques, as recent work links infant temperament attributes to
wakeful resting-state EEG microstates, providing a neural basis for early self-regulation and emotional reactivity   
[^10.7273_000007929].                                                                                                
+Recent advances in brain-to-language decoding have expanded the field from constrained recognition to text          
generation and streaming personalized speech, synthesizing developments across invasive and non-invasive measurements
while highlighting the need for shared benchmarks and longitudinal studies [^2609.27650]. To address session         
variability in invasive brain-machine interfaces, Task-Conditioned Latent Alignment (TCLA) stabilizes neural decoding
by learning a shared latent space aligned separately for each task condition, significantly reducing failure rates   
across cross-subject and long-term evaluations [^2609.27441]. In the realm of signal preprocessing, attention-guided 
conditional adversarial learning (P2P Trans) effectively suppresses electromyographic artifacts in single-channel    
EEG, demonstrating superior spectral restoration under severe contamination [^2609.26772]. Furthermore, modeling     
long-horizon EEG sequences benefits from neuroscience-inspired tokenization; Brain-Token Learning replaces fixed     
temporal windows with microstate-derived brain tokens, capturing quasi-stable large-scale brain states and improving 
representation learning across affective and clinical datasets [^2609.24324]. Optimizing cross-subject EEG decoding  
remains challenging due to gradient nonstationarity, but adaptive forgetting controllers like AFOR dynamically adjust
second-moment decay coefficients, yielding robust performance improvements over fixed optimizers [^2609.24233].      
Developmental neuroscience also explores these techniques, with proposed frameworks linking infant temperament       
attributes to wakeful resting-state EEG microstates to investigate early self-regulation and emotional reactivity    
[^10.7273_000007929].                                                                                                
                                                                                                                     
 ## Computational Neuroimaging and Foundation Models                                                                 
                                                                                                                     
 The integration of foundation models and scalable simulation is transforming structural and functional neuroimaging.
BrainFedFM introduces a dual-priority federated pretraining strategy for structural brain MRI, achieving             
state-of-the-art performance across diverse classification and segmentation tasks without pooling raw images         
[^2609.27611]. At the mechanistic level, CerebroSim enables scalable whole-brain spiking simulation at the           
100-trillion-synapse scale on supercomputers, utilizing delay-aware spike broadcast and sparse synapse storage       
compression to make biologically constrained human-brain models practical for disorder studies [^2609.27482]. Image  
reconstruction techniques are also advancing rapidly; Directional Total Variation-Regularized Implicit Neural        
Representations (DTV-INR) integrate coordinate-driven networks with anisotropic regularizers to achieve continuous   
super-resolution in clinical brain MRI, circumventing staircasing artifacts [^2609.25429]. Similarly,                
uncertainty-aware 3D residual wavelet diffusion models address memory bottlenecks in ultra low-field MRI             
super-resolution, generating per-voxel uncertainty maps that highlight anatomical ambiguity while preserving         
disease-relevant atrophy [^2609.25319]. Motion correction in magnetic resonance fingerprinting has been enhanced by  
compact spiral navigators, enabling sub-second motion estimation and improving quantitative map reliability under    
rapid, unpredictable motion [^2609.25313]. Methodologically, additive nonparametric regression frameworks            
incorporating Gaussian process priors effectively predict task-based brain activation maps by modeling intricate     
functional dependencies between structural, resting-state, and task-fMRI data [^2609.26867]. In developmental        
contexts, deep learning is shifting infant functional neuroimaging from group-level analyses toward reliable,        
individualized trajectory forecasting [^2609.26688]. Comparative studies using EEG encoding models reveal that vision
transformers sustain brain-predictive correspondence at deeper network layers better than CNNs, largely due to       
preserved patch-token representations [^2609.26512]. Additionally, functional causal discovery via conditional       
covariance ordering provides a distribution-free approach to estimating brain effective connectivity directed acyclic
graphs [^2609.27256].                                                                                                
                                                                                                                     
 ## Neurodegenerative Disorders and Brain Tumors                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
-Understanding and treating brain pathologies continues to drive methodological innovation. Low-level blast exposure 
in veterans has been linked to statistically significant increases in depression and mental health symptoms,         
independent of PTSD or alcohol use, suggesting cumulative overpressure accelerates neurodegenerative or affective    
decline [^2609.25279]. Automated segmentation of brain metastases, which are often small and heterogeneous, benefits 
from multi-architecture comparisons; transformer-based models like Primus and residual encoder variants show         
complementary strengths in aggregate Dice scores versus lesion-wise F1 metrics [^2609.24769]. Unsupervised anomaly   
detection in medical scans is being reformulated as a Bayesian inverse problem with diffusion priors, jointly        
inferring pseudo-healthy images and spatial anomaly masks to improve localization on FDG PET and FLAIR MRI           
[^2609.24265]. In Alzheimer's disease research, the amyloid precursor protein (APP) is increasingly viewed as a      
universal pro-survival molecule rather than merely a pathological substrate, raising critical questions about the    
safety of broad-spectrum APP suppression therapies [^10.5281_zenodo.20049820]. Liquid biopsy approaches for          
glioblastoma multiforme (GBM) are advancing through cfDNA analysis; nucleosome repositioning landscapes and          
repeat-based cfDNA profiling offer non-invasive diagnostic signatures that mirror tumor-specific chromatin           
reprogramming [^10.5526_err-00042862][^10.5526_err-00042900]. Spatially resolved single-cell atlases are mapping the 
tumor microenvironment in pan-cancer brain metastases, providing high-resolution insights into cellular heterogeneity
[^10.5281_zenodo.21816284][^10.5281_zenodo.21782885]. Therapeutic delivery across the blood-brain barrier is being   
addressed via lipid nanoparticle formulations encapsulating siRNA targeting TERT or loaded with anticancer           
phytochemicals, demonstrating robust gene silencing and cytotoxic effects in GBM cell lines                          
[^10.17030_uclan.thesis.00059253][^10.17030_uclan.thesis.00059033]. Furthermore, RET signaling pathways have been    
identified as mediators of estrogen receptor-positive breast cancer brain metastasis [^10.5281_zenodo.19501083],     
while secretomes and extracellular vesicles from neural crest-derived stem cells show promise in inhibiting GBM      
proliferation and modulating inflammatory microenvironments [^10.48683_1926.00128572]. Finally, aerobic exercise is  
proposed to reshape nitric oxide signaling toward adaptive profiles that may enhance mitophagy and provide           
neuroprotection in Parkinson's disease [^10.4103_mgr.medgasres-d-26-00008].                                          
